* How Jenkins works
** Purpose
*** Automation server for build, test, deploy
*** Hudson (2006) renamed Jenkins (January 2011)
*** jenkins.war core + 2300+ plugins
** Nodes
*** Controller: state, HTTP, scheduling
*** Built-in node: 0 executors
*** Agents and executors, labels
*** Clouds provision agents on demand
** Remoting
*** SSH launcher (controller connects)
*** Inbound TCP port (disabled by default)
*** Inbound WebSocket (since 2.217)
** Queue
*** Triggers and quiet period
*** Label match, free executor
*** NodeProvisioner, persisted queue
** Core model
*** Jenkins root object
*** Item, Job, Run, Action
*** Describable and Descriptor
*** @Extension and ExtensionList
** Plugins
*** .hpi / .jpi, one class loader each
*** Update Center, dependencies
** JENKINS_HOME
*** XML via XStream, no database
*** jobs/, builds/, plugins/, secrets/
** Web layer
*** Stapler: object graph = URL tree
*** Jelly / Groovy views
*** /api/json, CLI over WebSocket
** Pipeline
*** Jenkinsfile, Declarative vs Scripted
*** CPS: program.dat, survives restart
*** Groovy on controller, sh on agent
*** Shared libraries, multibranch
** Operations
*** JCasC: jenkins.yaml
*** Scaling: agents, more controllers
*** Jenkins infra: Helm chart, baked plugins
*** Security: separate article
@endmindmap
